{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Would that all the people of HaShem were prophets.</b> It is possible to say that Moses alluded here to the days of the Messiah. He said: Who would grant that that time arrive when all the people of HaShem would be prophets — for He, blessed be He, is destined to put His spirit upon them, and at that time the evil impulse will be annulled and there will be no jealousy there at all. And this is what is written (Joel 3:1): ״בעת ההיא אשפוך את רוחי על כל בשר״, and it is written (Isaiah 11:9): ״ומלאה הארץ דעה את ה׳ כמים לים מכסים״. And even though it has not been fulfilled in this world, it will be fulfilled in the time to come, in the era of perfection." "<b>And I found</b> an allusion to this in the book of Psalms (14), in the verse ״מי יתן מציון ישועת ישראל״. Why was this verse written twice in the book of Psalms — once in the first book and once in the second (53)? Rather, one the master said and one the disciple said. The master said (Deuteronomy 5:26): ״מי יתן והיה לבבם זה״, and the disciple said: ״מי יתן כל עם ה׳ נביאים״. In this world only the words of the master were fulfilled, but in the time to come both of them are fulfilled. The words of the master — from where? As it is said (Ezekiel 36:26): ״ונתתי לכם לב חדש״. The words of the disciple — from where? As it is said (Joel 3:1): ״אשפוך את רוחי על כל בשר״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:36.731377534Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}